  "narrative": "The enrichment analysis identifies **Glycine, serine and threonine metabolism (KEGG map00260)** as the dominant affected pathway (FDR 0.0, fold enrichment 632×), with eight input metabolites confirmed as pathway members: AMP, L-cysteine, L-threonine, phosphoserine, glycine, L-cystathionine, S-adenosylhomocysteine, and guanidoacetic acid. A secondary enriched candidate is **Trans-sulfuration, one-carbon metabolism and related pathways (Wikipathways WP2525)**, which shares cystathionine, cysteine, and SAH with the sulfur amino acid arm of one-carbon metabolism.\n\n**Key driver compounds.** L-Threonine (C00188) and phosphoserine (C01005) are the primary inputs, feeding the serine–glycine axis. Phosphoserine sits upstream of glycine via a two-step KEGG path (phosphoserine → serine → glycine), establishing it as a driver of glycine production. L-Cystathionine (C02291) is a downstream node in the trans-sulfuration pathway; KEGG reaction graph analysis confirms L-cysteine → cystathionine (via cystathionine β-synthase). This links the one-carbon pool to the sulfur amino acid arm of the pathway. S-Adenosylhomocysteine (C00021) anchors the methyl-donor cycle, and its accumulation is consistent with disturbed methyl-group recycling.\n\n**Biological significance.** The convergence of glycine, serine, threonine, cysteine, and SAH suggests a broad perturbation of one-carbon metabolism and methyl-group transfer. Dysregulation of this network impacts nucleotide synthesis (via glycine and serine), glutathione production (via cysteine), and epigenetic methylation capacity (via SAH). Guanidoacetic acid maps to creatine synthesis, an arginine-guanidino pathway derivative that also depends on methyl-group availability.\n\n**Verified directional relationships.** Phosphoserine → Glycine (KEGG path C01005 → C00065 → C00037, 2 hops) and L-Cysteine → L-Cystathionine (KEGG path C00097 → C00022 → C00065 → C02291, 3 hops) are confirmed upstream-to-downstream relationships in the reaction graph. Niacinamide (C00153) and N1-acetylspermidine (C00612) represent additional metabolic nodes (NAD⁺ salvage and polyamine catabolism) that intersect with one-carbon flux but are not primary drivers of map00260.",
